Q1. What is typescript ?
    Answer:
        Typescript is the superset of Javascript which supports OOPS, with the help of OOPS, we can write readable, reusable and concise codes.
        Typescript check errors on compile time only, that's why the errors will reduce on typescript application.

Q2. What is the difference between interface and type in TypeScript?
    Answer:
        Interface
        -----------
        -> Interfaces can extend other interfaces to combine multiple structures.

                interface A {
                  name: string;
                }

                interface B extends A {
                  age: number;
                }

        Types
        ------
                type A = { name: string };
                type B = { age: number };

                type Person = A & B;

        Also, type supports union (|), which allows a variable to hold multiple data types.

        -> Interface is best for object design and extension, while type is more powerful for unions and complex combinations.


Q3. any & unknown
    Answer:
        Any
        ----
            -> any disables TypeScript type checking.
            -> You can assign anything and perform any operation.
                    let value: any = "Amir";

                    value.toUpperCase(); ✅
                    value(); ✅
                    value = 100; ✅

        unknown
        --------
            -> We are not aware about the data type initially, so before using it, we must check what type it is.
                    let value: unknown = "Amir";

                    if (typeof value === "string") {
                      console.log(value.toUpperCase());
                    }

Q4: What is never type in TypeScript?
    Answer:
        -> never is a special type in TypeScript that represents a value that never occurs.

                Eg:
                    -> 1. Function that never returns
                            function throwError(message: string): never {
                              throw new Error(message);
                            }

                    -> 2. Infinite loop (never ends)
                            function infiniteLoop(): never {
                              while (true) {}
                            }

Q5: What are Generics in TypeScript?
    Answer:
        -> Generics in TypeScript allow us to create reusable and type-safe components by using a placeholder type instead of fixed types.
        -> Generics provide flexibility, reusability, and strong type safety, making code scalable in large applications.

                function pair<T, U>(a: T, b: U) {
                  return [a, b];
                }
                pair<string, number>("Age", 27);

Q6: What is keyof in TypeScript?
    Answer:
        -> keyof is a TypeScript operator that returns a union of all keys (property names) of an object type.

        It is mainly used for:
        ✅ type-safe property access
        ✅ dynamic key checking
        ✅ generic constraints

        interface User {
          name: string;
          age: number;
        }
        type UserKeys = keyof User;

Q7: What is typeof in TypeScript?
    Answer:
        typeof is an operator in TypeScript used to extract the type of a variable or object.
            const config = {
              apiUrl: "http://localhost:8080",
              timeout: 5000
            };

            type ConfigType = typeof config;

            type Keys = keyof typeof config;
            "apiUrl" | "timeout"

Q8: What are Utility Types in TypeScript? (Partial, Pick, Omit)
    Answer:
        Utility Types are built-in generic types provided by TypeScript to transform existing types easily.
        They help write cleaner and reusable code.

                1. Partial<T>
                        interface User {
                          name: string;
                          age: number;
                        }

                        let user1: Partial<User> = {
                          name: "Amir"
                        };

                    -> ✅ age optional हो गया

                2. Pick<T, K>
                        type UserName = Pick<User, "name">;

                        let u: UserName = {
                          name: "Amir"
                        };
                    -> Select only specific properties from a type.

                3. Omit<T, K>
                        type UserWithoutAge = Omit<User, "age">;

                        let u2: UserWithoutAge = {
                          name: "Amir"
                        };

                        -> Remove specific properties from a type.

                4. Readonly<T>
                        type ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>;

                        let u3: ReadonlyUser = {
                          name: "Amir",
                          age: 27
                        };

                        u3.age = 30; ❌ Error

                        -> Makes properties read-only.

                Short Notes Version
                ---------------------
                -> Partial → all optional
                -> Pick → select keys
                -> Omit → remove keys
                -> Readonly → no modification

